course: biology
lesson: 122
title: Looney Tunes: the real roadrunner and the real coyote
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Looney Tunes the Road Runner zooms off in a cloud of dust, says «meep meep», and Wile E. Coyote never catches him, however many ACME rockets he buys.
explain: The real greater roadrunner is a desert bird of the cuckoo family that would rather run than fly, at about 30 kilometres an hour, which is fast for a bird its size.
explain: But a real coyote can run more than twice as fast, and sometimes a coyote really does catch a roadrunner, though the bird often dodges into a bush in time.
explain: Real coyotes use no rockets or traps: they stalk, chase and pounce, live in many cities without being seen, and call to their families with yips and howls at night.
explain: And the biggest cartoon fib of all is the cliff: nothing hangs in the air before it falls, because gravity pulls the moment there is no ground below you.
fact: A greater roadrunner can run at about 30 km per hour, while a coyote can run more than twice as fast, and coyotes sometimes catch and eat roadrunners.
fact: Real roadrunners do not say «meep meep»; they make soft cooing sounds and loud rattles by clattering their beaks.
fact: Roadrunners eat lizards, insects, scorpions, mice, small birds and even snakes, including young rattlesnakes, which a roadrunner kills by grabbing and whacking their heads against a rock.
fact: Roadrunners can fly, but only short and clumsily; they have short rounded wings and strong legs, and they belong to the cuckoo family.
fact: On cold desert mornings a roadrunner lifts its back feathers to warm a patch of dark skin in the sun, since dark skin takes in more heat.
fact: A roadrunner has two toes pointing forward and two pointing back on each foot, so its footprints look like the letter X, and you cannot tell which way the bird went; some native peoples of the South-west saw the X track as a sign of protection.
fact: Real coyotes do not use tools; they hunt by stalking, chasing and pouncing (for a mouse in grass or snow a coyote leaps high and pounces down on it), alone, in pairs or in small family groups.
fact: Coyotes now live in many North American cities, including Chicago and Los Angeles, eating rats, mice, fruit and rubbish and moving about at night.
fact: Coyotes howl, yip and bark to keep in touch with family and to tell other coyotes the area is taken, and two coyotes can sound like a whole pack.
fact: Gravity pulls at once when there is no ground below; the coyote's pause in the air is «cartoon physics», put there so the audience has time to laugh.
fact: Roadrunners and coyotes really share the deserts of the south-western United States and Mexico, resting in shade at midday.
fact: Roadrunners get most of the water they need from the animals they eat and rarely drink.
exercise: Who is faster in real life, a roadrunner or a coyote? | The coyote, more than twice as fast | The roadrunner, by far, just as in the cartoon | They are exactly as fast | Neither can run, they only walk
exercise: About how fast can a greater roadrunner run? | About 30 km per hour | Only about 3 km per hour | About 300 km per hour | About 130 km per hour
exercise: What sounds does a real roadrunner make? | Soft coos and rattles made by clattering its beak | «Meep meep», just like the cartoon bird on the road | Loud howls at the moon | Nothing at all, it is a completely silent bird
exercise: What does a roadrunner eat? | Lizards, insects, scorpions, mice and even small snakes | Only the sweet red fruit that grows on desert cactus plants | Only seeds and grass | Only dead animals it finds
exercise: Why does a roadrunner usually run instead of flying away? | Its wings are short and its legs strong, so running costs less effort | It has no wings at all | Its feathers are too heavy and wet to lift it off the hot ground | It is afraid of the sky and of the eagles that fly there
exercise: Which bird family does the roadrunner belong to? | The cuckoo family | The ostrich family | The chicken family | The big eagle family
exercise: Why does a roadrunner fluff up its back feathers on a cold morning? | To warm a patch of dark skin in the sun | To look bigger and more frightening to a coyote | To shake off the sand | To dry off after a bath
exercise: Why do roadrunner footprints look like an X? | Two toes point forward and two point back | It drags its long tail behind it across the sand | It hops on one foot | It crosses its legs when walking
exercise: How do real coyotes hunt? | By stalking, chasing and pouncing | With traps and rockets | By digging pits for prey | By waiting for food to fall from trees
exercise: How does a coyote catch a mouse in grass or snow? | It jumps high and pounces down on it | It sniffs it out and waits all day | It digs a long tunnel to it | It scares it into a pond
exercise: Where do coyotes live today? | In wild places and in many big cities too | Only in deep forests far away from any people | Only in zoos | Only in hot deserts
exercise: Why do coyotes yip and howl at night? | To keep in touch with family and claim the area | Because they are scared of the dark and call for help | To call the moon down | Because they are hungry for cactus
exercise: What really happens when something runs off a cliff? | It falls at once, as gravity pulls straight away | It hangs in the air until it looks down and sees the drop | It floats down slowly like a leaf | It can run back if it is fast enough
exercise: Why do animators make the coyote hang in the air before he falls? | To give us time to laugh | Because that is how gravity works | Because coyotes are very light | Because the desert wind holds him up for a moment
exercise: Where do roadrunners and coyotes really live together? | Deserts of the south-western United States and Mexico | The sandy Sahara Desert in the north of Africa | The rainforests of Brazil | The cold forests of Canada
exercise: How does a roadrunner get most of its water? | From the animals it eats | From the cactus flowers only | By drinking at a pond every hour | From the cold morning snow
test: «coyotes can never catch roadrunners, cartoon proves it» What is right? | Coyotes are faster and sometimes catch them, though roadrunners often dodge into bushes | True, roadrunners are the fastest animals on Earth, faster than any cheetah or car | True, coyotes do not eat birds | True, roadrunners fly away every time
test: «lol 2 roadrunners, how many toes pointing backwards altogether» Each foot has two back toes. How many? | 8 | 4 | 2 | 16
test: «3 coyotes + 1 roadrunner, how many legs» How many? | 14 | 16 | 12 | 10
test: Your friend says roadrunners cannot fly at all. What is right? | They can fly, but only short and clumsily | Right, they have no wings | Right, they are a kind of ostrich | Wrong, they fly long distances every day
test: «what if I ran off a cliff and didn't look down, would I hover» What is right? | No, you would fall at once; never try it | Yes, for a second or two | Yes, if you kept moving your legs and running in the air | Only if you held an umbrella
test: «saw a coyote by the bus stop in LA, escaped from a zoo or wot» What is most likely? | Coyotes live wild in many cities and move about at night | It must have escaped from a zoo or a wildlife park nearby | It was surely a lost pet dog | Coyotes never come near people's towns
test: A cartoon roadrunner sips water from a stream. What do real roadrunners do? | Get most water from their food and rarely drink | Drink a whole bucketful of water every hour in the heat | Drink only rain from cactus flowers | Cannot swallow water at all
test: «2 coyotes yipping out there, sounds like 20, run for it» What is right? | Two coyotes' voices overlap and bounce, so they can sound like a pack | There must really be twenty of them out there in the dark | Coyotes always call in groups of ten | It must be wolves, coyotes are silent
test: A roadrunner meets a young rattlesnake. What might really happen? | The roadrunner may kill it by whacking its head against a rock | The roadrunner always runs away, because all big snakes eat birds | The snake always eats the roadrunner | They ignore each other completely and walk on past
test: Why might native peoples of the South-west have seen the roadrunner's X track as a protection sign? | You cannot tell which way the bird went | It shows the bird brings good luck to any family | It points to water | It frightens away coyotes
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the relative speeds of the greater roadrunner and the coyote. | The coyote is capable of more than twice the speed of the roadrunner and occasionally captures it | The roadrunner invariably outpaces the coyote by a considerable margin | The two species attain identical maximum speeds | The coyote is incapable of capturing any species of bird
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the footprint of the roadrunner resembles the letter X. | Each foot bears two toes directed forward and two directed backward | The bird drags its tail transversely across its tracks | The bird proceeds exclusively by hopping upon one foot | The bird crosses its legs at each step
exam: It is depicted in an animated film that a coyote remains suspended in the air beyond the edge of a cliff. Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the matter? | Gravity acts without delay, and a body unsupported from below commences to fall at once | A body remains suspended in the air until it perceives the absence of ground beneath it | A body unsupported from below descends at a constant slow rate | Gravity acts upon a body only after an interval of several seconds
exam: Ascertain the principal means by which the coyote obtains its prey. | By stalking, pursuit and pouncing | By the employment of manufactured traps | By the excavation of pits beneath the prey | By awaiting the fall of prey from trees
